{:nav-title "Relationships"
 :content
 [:div
  [:h1 "Relationships"]
  [:p "So far every query has looked at one kind of thing, a Pokemon, and its attributes. "
   "Datomic can also connect entities to each other. "
   "Our database has a second kind of entity, the " [:strong "types"] " like Fire and Water, "
   "along with which types are strong against which. Water is strong against Fire, for example."]
  [:p "Each type points at the types it is strong against. That takes an attribute whose value "
   "is another entity, a " [:strong "reference"] ", declared with " [:code ":db.type/ref"] ":"]
  [:ui/code "clojure"
   "(d/transact conn {:tx-data [{:db/ident :type/name
                             :db/valueType :db.type/string
                             :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one
                             :db/unique :db.unique/identity}

                            {:db/ident :type/strong-against
                             :db/valueType :db.type/ref
                             :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/many}]})"]
  [:p "A type is strong against several others, so the cardinality is many. "
   [:code ":db/unique :db.unique/identity"] " makes " [:code ":type/name"] " the type's natural key: "
   "at most one type entity can have a given name, and Datomic enforces it. "
   "The database also has " [:code ":type/weak-against"] " (half damage) and "
   [:code ":type/no-effect-on"] " (no damage)."]

  [:h2 "Asserting a reference with a lookup ref"]
  [:p "A unique attribute like " [:code ":type/name"] " lets you identify an entity without knowing its "
   "entity id, with a " [:strong "lookup ref"] ": a two-element vector like "
   [:code "[:type/name \"Fire\"]"] " that stands in for whichever entity has that value."]
  [:p "Lookup refs let you connect two entities in one transaction, using only values you already know. "
   "This adds a new strength for Water without looking up any ids:"]
  [:ui/code "clojure"
   "(d/transact conn {:tx-data [{:db/id [:type/name \"Water\"]
                            :type/strong-against [:type/name \"Fire\"]}]})"]
  [:p "Entity ids are assigned by Datomic and can differ between databases, so code shouldn't store them "
   "outside it. A unique attribute is stable, and a lookup ref lets you use it anywhere an entity id is "
   "expected, including as the value of a reference attribute, as here."]

  [:h2 "Following a reference"]
  [:p "The value of a reference attribute is an entity id. "
   "That means a variable can be an entity in one clause and a value in the next. "
   "Read the second clause below as \"" [:code "?attacker"] " is strong against " [:code "?fire"] "\":"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name
    :where
    [?fire :type/name "Fire"]
    [?attacker :type/strong-against ?fire]
    [?attacker :type/name ?name]]]

  [:h2 "Both directions"]
  [:p "The same attribute answers the opposite question. "
   "Which position the known entity takes decides the direction. "
   "Here Fire is on the left, so we get the types Fire is strong against:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name
    :where
    [?fire :type/name "Fire"]
    [?fire :type/strong-against ?defender]
    [?defender :type/name ?name]]]

  [:h2 "Joining by value"]
  [:p "A Pokemon's types are plain strings, like " [:code "\"Ghost\""] ", and a type entity has the same string in "
   [:code ":type/name"] ". We didn't need a reference between them. "
   "As we saw in " [:a {:href "/anatomy-of-a-query"} "Anatomy of a query"] ", two clauses that use the same variable "
   "have to agree on it. That works on any equal values, whether it is an entity id or a string."]
  [:p "This is a shortcut for the tutorial. In a real schema " [:code ":pokemon/type"]
   " would usually be a " [:code ":db.type/ref"] " to the type entity, which gives "
   "referential integrity (no typos that silently join to nothing) and lets "
   [:code "pull"] " follow it directly."]
  [:p "Let's use that to answer a bigger question: which Pokemon hit Gengar super-effectively? "
   "We'll build the query one step at a time. First, Gengar's types, as strings:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?defending-name
    :where
    [?gengar :pokemon/name "Gengar"]
    [?gengar :pokemon/type ?defending-name]]]
  [:p "Next, the type entities those strings name. " [:code "?defending-name"] " appears in both the "
   [:code ":pokemon/type"] " clause and the new " [:code ":type/name"] " clause, so they have to agree, "
   "and the join is by value:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?defending ?defending-name
    :where
    [?gengar :pokemon/name "Gengar"]
    [?gengar :pokemon/type ?defending-name]
    [?defending :type/name ?defending-name]]]
  [:p "Now the types strong against them. This is the reference from the start of the chapter, "
   "from the attacking type to the defending one:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?attacking-name
    :where
    [?gengar :pokemon/name "Gengar"]
    [?gengar :pokemon/type ?defending-name]
    [?defending :type/name ?defending-name]
    [?attacking :type/strong-against ?defending]
    [?attacking :type/name ?attacking-name]]]
  [:p "The last step goes back to Pokemon: the ones that have one of those types. "
   "The query has hopped across three kinds of links: from Gengar's type strings to their type entities "
   "(by value), to the types strong against those (by reference), "
   "and back to the Pokemon that have such a type (by value):"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name
    :where
    [?gengar :pokemon/name "Gengar"]
    [?gengar :pokemon/type ?defending-name]
    [?defending :type/name ?defending-name]
    [?attacking :type/strong-against ?defending]
    [?attacking :type/name ?attacking-name]
    [?e :pokemon/type ?attacking-name]
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]]]
  [:p "\"Super-effectively\" is a simplification worth naming: a real Pokemon attacks with " [:em "moves"]
   ", whose type doesn't have to match its own, and having two types multiplies the bonus rather than "
   "just adding an attacker. What the rule actually finds is \"has a type strong against one of Gengar's types.\" "
   "That is also why Gengar, Haunter and Gastly show up in their own results: the type chart lists Ghost "
   "as strong against Ghost, and all three are Ghost type."]

  [:h2 "Negation across relationships"]
  [:p [:code "not-join"] " works across relationships too. "
   "Which types have no Pokemon at all? "
   "Only " [:code "?name"] " is shared between the " [:code "not-join"] " and the rest of the query:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name
    :where
    [?type :type/name ?name]
    (not-join [?name]
              [?pokemon :pokemon/type ?name])]]
  [:ui/try-tip
   [:p "Change " [:code "\"Gengar\""] " to another Pokemon, like " [:code "\"Charizard\""] ", in the last join. "
    "Or in the first query, change " [:code "\"Fire\""] " to " [:code "\"Ghost\""] " and "
    [:code ":type/strong-against"] " to " [:code ":type/no-effect-on"] " to see which types can't touch Ghost."]]]}
